UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 3: Recording and comparing results', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup stays the same.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTwo students spin a fair 2-colour spinner 10 times each. One gets 7 red, one gets 4 red. What can you say?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDeclaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompare two experiment result tables (yours vs a partner or given set). Make a combined graph. Write three comparison sentences including one about variation.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup stays the same.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-CHAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compare two experiment result tables (yours vs a partner or given set). Make a combined graph. Write three comparison sentences including one about variation. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for record; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation.. Correction uses Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture.. Why: restores accurate record.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses record with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with record, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for record, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about record, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for record. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture","Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Two students spin a fair 2-colour spinner 10 times each. One gets 7 red, one gets 4 red. What can you say?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for record; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup","Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture","Careful use of compare and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with record?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named compare and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation","Name compare and explain why it matters only, without linking it to record","Apply record to new material and name the compare and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies record with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on record?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught record method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Read 2 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 2 and 10 as interchangeable","Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture","Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation"],"question":"Using the method for record, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a record problem with numbers such as 2 and 10, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same record method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 2 and 10 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation"],"question":"Solve a fresh record problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation","Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup","Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture","Label units when comparing 2 with 10"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for record?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for record, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses record with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for record; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about record, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with record, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation.. Correction uses Both results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture.. Why: restores accurate record.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w39-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise record.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Statistics and Probability","success_criteria":["I can use record with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify compare and explain why it matters.","I can practise sample independently on a new prompt.","I can notice variation needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup stays the same.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about record with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGroup A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by side; do not accuse unfairness from one small difference alone.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTwo students spin a fair 2-colour spinner 10 times each. One gets 7 red, one gets 4 red. What can you say?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture.","explain":"Recording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup stays the same.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Comparing experiment samples.","caption":"Variation is expected.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Compare</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">two result tables</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Graph</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">side by side</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Group A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by si Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Group A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by si Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about record with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nGroup A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by side; do not accuse unfairness from one small difference alone. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Sample size idea.","caption":"Three rolls ≠ proof broken.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair die</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different tallies possible</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">More trials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clearer picture</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about record with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDeclaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation. Here is a more productive model: Group A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by side; do not accuse unfairness from one small difference alone.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBoth results possible; small samples vary; more trials give a clearer picture.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Declaring a die “broken” after three rolls ignores natural variation. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about record with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRecording and comparing results means using tables or graphs and noticing that samples vary even when the chance setup stays the same. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 3: Recording and comparing results","unit_number":10,"unit_title":"Data, chance and investigation","week":39,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nGroup A rolled 4 sixes in 20 rolls; Group B rolled 1 six — both possible with a fair die. Compare frequencies side by side; do not accuse unfairness from one small difference alone.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Comparing experiment samples.","caption":"Variation is expected.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Compare</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">two result tables</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Graph</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">side by side</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Sample size idea.","caption":"Three rolls ≠ proof broken.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fair die</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different tallies possible</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">More trials</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clearer picture</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-mat-y3-w39-l3' AND family_id IS NULL;
